A||||5||72vinman||Stripping paint from bumber??||||5||
Z||000000||72vinman||10-11-2001||04:52 PM||||Besides sanding, is there any other way to strip the paint from my front bumber (aero nose). I need to repaint it but it has too much old paint on there. I was hoping for some sort of chemical peel that is often used in wood refinishing. Does this exist? Please help I want to get my car back together before the snow comes. Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||fropuff||10-11-2001||06:25 PM||||Well, I used chemical stripper on my first fascia.  Notice I said first.  It did a good job taking the paint off, but also makes ripples in the plastic material.  It was made by Bulldog, and it's in a yellow can.  Maybe something else would work...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||10-11-2001||07:17 PM||||There is a product available to strip the flexiable bumpers. Read the labels.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||10-11-2001||08:24 PM||||in my shop, I never use any of the chemical stripers in fiberglass or plastic parts. First try taking it to a power car wash and see if you can blow it off with that. Otherwise I sand them. If you have any holes,splits or cracks strippers will stay in there and mess up your paint job.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tigger||10-11-2001||09:25 PM||||Could have it media blasted...  Don't recommend using any chemical strippers.<P>If you start out sanding with 220 or coarser paper you can speed up the time cutting through the layers, then back off to 320 or 400.  Be careful if you use a DA sander, you can strip it faster but with all of the contours in the bumper it's easy to put flats in the edges.||||reg||1
Z||000005||72vinman||10-12-2001||12:10 AM||||Thanks, for the tips guys. I guess I will be spending my day off tomorrow sanding it down. Take care||||reg||1
